            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.112,
            0.848,
            0.135
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.2 Solution of Linear Equation in Two Variables"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.146,
            0.882,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In earlier grades, we have seen how to solve linear equations in one variable. What can we say about the solution of a linear equation involving two variables?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.215,
            0.579,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.2.1 Algebraic Meaning of Solution"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.247,
            0.881,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "solution",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of a linear equation in two variables is a pair of values of x and y which satisfy the given equation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.287,
            0.596,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us consider the equation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x + 2y = 12"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.306,
            0.881,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here, x = 2 and y = 3 is a solution because substituting the values of x and y into the given equation satisfies the equation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.344,
            0.576,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x + 2y = (3 \\times 2) + (2 \\times 3) = 12."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.364,
            0.881,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A solution can be written compactly as a pair of numbers. For example, the solution x = 2 and y = 3 can be written as (2, 3). The order of the numbers in this pair matters: the first number is the x-value, and the second number is the y-value. Hence we call this an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ordered pair",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.44,
            0.88,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, (4, 0) is also a solution of the equation 3x + 2y = 12 since substituting x = 4 and y = 0 leads to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x + 2y = (3 \\times 4) + (2 \\times 0) = 12"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.478,
            0.538,
            0.496
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What about the ordered pair (1, 3)?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.497,
            0.874,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We see that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x+2y=3\\times \\frac{1}{2}+2\\times \\frac{1}{3}=9\\ne \\underline{12}.\\ \\underline{S}0,\\ (1,3)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is not a solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.534,
            0.4,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.568,
            0.852,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( 1 , \\frac { 9 } { 2 } \\right) , \\left( 0 , 6 \\right) , \\widehat { \\left( 4 , 1 \\right) }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{21}{4} \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " solutions of the equation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x + 2y = 12?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.613,
            0.798,
            0.634
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you find any other solution? How many solutions can you find?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.65,
            0.882,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose we are given a linear equation in two variables. For any value u that we assign to x, we can find a value v for y such that (u, v) is a solution. We first substitute u for x in the equation. This gives us a new linear equation in y that can be solved to find v. This allows us to generate an infinite number of solutions to the original equation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.757,
            0.658,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.2.2 Geometric Visualisation of Solutions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.789,
            0.881,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider all the solutions of a linear equation in two variables, expressed as ordered pairs. What do we get when we plot the points corresponding to these ordered pairs?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.847,
            0.643,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The equation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x + 2y = 12"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " can be rewritten as"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.867,
            0.822,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2y = - 3x + 12   or   y = - \\frac{3}{2}x + \\frac{12}{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " which is the same as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = - \\frac{3}{2}x + 6"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
